00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.220 align:start position:0%
federal regulators are warning airlines  about a potentially dangerous issue  affecting a handful of Boeing passenger  planes the FAA and Boeing say faulty  sensors on new 737 max jets can cause  the plane to go into a steep dive the  warning comes just over a week after a  Boeing 737 max plane crash in Indonesia  killing all 189 people on board Kris Van  Cleave reports the FAA is emergency  action orders Airlines to ensure 737 max  pilots are properly trained in the event  the plane goes into an uncommanded dive  similar to what lion Air flight 610  experienced before crashing last week  this is pretty significant Chris Ross  aimer is a retired airline captain who  flew an older version of the 737 I'm  actually surprised that the FA didn't  ground these max the aircraft because  when they issue an ad as you mentioned  that's an emergency situation the  horizontal stabilizer are those small  wings just below the tail flaps at the  rear known as the elevator can trim or  adjust the angle of the planes nose up  or down if the sensors that help control  the trim malfunction the 737 max could  automatically put itself into a dive  forcing pilots to react quickly to  override that system and pull up if you  let it continuously trim trim trim trim  trim trim trim trim so it's gonna happen  pretty fast  it could happen very fast yes the FAA  estimates 245 of the new 737 max Jets  are in use worldwide 49 are currently  flown by three US carriers there have  been no reports of malfunctioning  sensors on 737 max planes in the u.s.  early indications from the recovered  flight data recorder in Indonesia show  that sensor had malfunctioned on the  planes three previous flights leading up  to Lion Air 610 s crash the airline  maintained a mechanical issue was fixed  prior to the doomed flight we don't know  exactly what happened at I cockpit did I  actually get a runaway stabiliser if  they did why didn't they immediately  correct it and take care of it Boeing  called it's bulletin part of its usual  process however  it is unusual for a directive like this  to be issued for a brand new type of  airliner aviation experts tell CBS News  this kind of malfunction alone should  not have resulted in a crash adding US  airline pilots practice for this type of  issue for CBS this morning Saturday Kris  Van Cleave Washington Chris thank you
 

